 and exhibit preferences for lesions in the telomeric sequence context","volume":"288","author":"Zhou","year":"2013","journal-title":"J. Biol. Chem."},{"key":"ref_97","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"226","DOI":"10.1021\/acscentsci.5b00202","article-title":"A role for the fifth G-track in G-quadruplex forming oncogene promoter sequences during oxidative stress: Do these \u201cspare tires\u201d have an evolved function?","volume":"1","author":"Fleming","year":"2015","journal-title":"ACS Cent. Sci."},{"key":"ref_98","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"4039","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkv252","article-title":"The NEIL glycosylases remove oxidized guanine lesions from telomeric and promoter quadruplex DNA structures","volume":"43","author":"Zhou","year":"2015","journal-title":"Nucleic Acids Res."},{"key":"ref_99","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"155","DOI":"10.1002\/bies.20523","article-title":"Physiological relevance of telomeric G-quadruplex formation: A potential drug target","volume":"29","author":"Oganesian","year":"2007","journal-title":"Bioessays"}],"container-title":["International Journal of Molecular Sciences"],"language":"en","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/1422-0067\/23\/24\/16020\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,11]],"date-time":"2025-10-11T01:42:33Z","timestamp":1760146953000},"score":72.62566,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/1422-0067\/23\/24\/16020"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,16]]},"references-count":99,"journal-issue":{"issue":"24","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2022,12]]}},"alternative-id":["ijms232416020"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/ijms232416020","ISSN":["1422-0067"],"issn-type":[{"value":"1422-0067","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,16]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,31]],"date-time":"2026-10-31T15:16:43Z","timestamp":1793459803113,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":87,"publisher":"Oxford University Press (OUP)","issue":"3","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2021,1,20]],"date-time":"2021-01-20T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1611100800000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":1,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc\/4.0\/"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100000038","name":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["327028-09"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["327028-09"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100000038","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,2,22]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n               <jats:p>G-quadruplexes (G4s) are four-stranded, guanine-rich nucleic acid structures that can influence a variety of biological processes such as the transcription and translation of genes and DNA replication. In many cases, a single G4-forming nucleic acid sequence can adopt multiple different folded conformations that interconvert on biologically relevant timescales, entropically stabilizing the folded state. The coexistence of different folded conformations also suggests that there are multiple pathways leading from the unfolded to the folded state ensembles, potentially modulating the folding rate and biological activity. We have developed an experimental method for quantifying the contributions of individual pathways to the folding of conformationally heterogeneous G4s that is based on mutagenesis, thermal hysteresis kinetic experiments and global analysis, and validated our results using photocaged kinetic NMR experiments. We studied the regulatory Pu22 G4 from the c-myc oncogene promoter, which adopts at least four distinct folded isomers. We found that the presence of four parallel pathways leads to a 2.5-fold acceleration in folding;\u00a0that is, the effective folding rate from the unfolded to folded ensembles is 2.5 times as large as the rate constant for the fastest individual pathway. Since many G4 sequences can adopt many more than four isomers, folding accelerations of more than an order of magnitude are possible via this mechanism.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkaa1286","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2020,12,27]],"date-time":"2020-12-27T12:07:02Z","timestamp":1609070822000},"page":"1247-1262","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":21,"title":["Parallel reaction pathways accelerate folding of a guanine quadruplex"],"prefix":"10.1093","volume":"49","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2095-9860","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Robert W","family":"Harkness","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Molecular Genetics, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON M5S 1A8, Canada"},{"name":"Department of Chemistry, McGill University, Montreal, QC H3A 0B8, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Christopher","family":"Hennecker","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemistry, McGill University, Montreal, QC H3A 0B8, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"J Tassilo","family":"Gr\u00fcn","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Organic Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Goethe University,\u00a0Frankfurt am Main 60438, Germany"},{"name":"Center of Biomolecular Magnetic Resonance (BMRZ), Goethe University,\u00a0Frankfurt am Main 60438, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anja","family":"Bl\u00fcmler","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Organic Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Goethe University,\u00a0Frankfurt am Main 60438, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alexander","family":"Heckel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Organic Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Goethe University,\u00a0Frankfurt am Main 60438, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5693-7909","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Harald","family":"Schwalbe","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Organic Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Goethe University,\u00a0Frankfurt am Main 60438, Germany"},{"name":"Center of Biomolecular Magnetic Resonance (BMRZ), Goethe University,\u00a0Frankfurt am Main 60438, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3767-1225","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Anthony\u00a0K","family":"Mittermaier","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemistry, McGill University, Montreal, QC H3A 0B8, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"286","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2021,1,19]]},"reference":[{"key":"2021022123592935800_B1","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"5482","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkn517","article-title":"Stability and kinetics of G-quadruplex structures","volume":"36","author":"Lane","year":"2008","journal-title":"Nucleic Acids Res."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"406","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkl1057","article-title":"G-quadruplexes in promoters throughout the human genome","volume":"35","author":"Huppert","year":"2007","journal-title":"Nucleic Acids Res."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B3","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"1544","DOI":"10.1016\/j.bbapap.2017.06.012","article-title":"G-quadruplex dynamics","volume":"1865","author":"Harkness","year":"2017","journal-title":"Biochim. Biophys. Acta"},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B4","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"2908","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gki609","article-title":"Prevalence of quadruplexes in the human genome","volume":"33","author":"Huppert","year":"2005","journal-title":"Nucleic Acids Res."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B5","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"5017","DOI":"10.1021\/ja310251r","article-title":"Bulges in G-Quadruplexes: broadening the definition of G-Quadruplex-Forming sequences","volume":"135","author":"Mukundan","year":"2013","journal-title":"J. Am. Chem. Soc."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B6","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"910","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkv1357","article-title":"G-quadruplexes with (4n - 1) guanines in the G-tetrad core: formation of a G-triad\u00b7water complex and implication for small-molecule binding","volume":"44","author":"Heddi","year":"2016","journal-title":"Nucleic Acids Res."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B7","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"1009","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkp1029","article-title":"Structure of a two-G-tetrad intramolecular G-quadruplex formed by a variant human telomeric sequence in K(+) solution: insights into the interconversion of human telomeric G-quadruplex structures","volume":"38","author":"Zhang","year":"2010","journal-title":"Nucleic Acids Res."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B8","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"4877","DOI":"10.1021\/bi0300985","article-title":"Stability and structure of telomeric DNA sequences forming quadruplexes containing four G-Tetrads with different topological arrangements","volume":"43","author":"Petraccone","year":"2004","journal-title":"Biochemistry"},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B9","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"15679","DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-018-33944-4","article-title":"Case studies on potential G-quadruplex-forming sequences from the bacterial orders Deinococcales and Thermales derived from a survey of published genomes","volume":"8","author":"Ding","year":"2018","journal-title":"Sci. Rep."},{"key":"2021022123592935800_B10","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"3862","DOI":"10.1093\/nar\/gkz179","article-title":"Whole genome experimental maps of DNA G-quadruplexes in multiple species","volume":"47","author":"Marsico","year":"2019","journal-title":"Nucleic Acid